{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1777645769674,"sessionID":"ses_21c0e7915ffeGs97vKkGBk72cn","part":{"id":"prt_de3f1c7be001K52NY8n07jE8ob","messageID":"msg_de3f1b5450017XwlCvKrLYp5iE","sessionID":"ses_21c0e7915ffeGs97vKkGBk72cn","type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_e24ad2545e3a400289ae2cbf","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/jinxu/opencode-spawn-pilot/outputs/opencode_spawn_pilot/comparison_v12_single/.prompt_musique_2hop__53433_59035_1777645357.txt","limit":15,"offset":54},"output":"<path>/home/jinxu/opencode-spawn-pilot/outputs/opencode_spawn_pilot/comparison_v12_single/.prompt_musique_2hop__53433_59035_1777645357.txt</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>54: On 6 February 1840, Hobson and about forty Māori chiefs signed the Treaty of Waitangi at Waitangi in the Bay of Islands. Copies of the Treaty were subsequently taken around the country to be signed by other chiefs. A significant number refused to sign or were not asked but, in total, more than five hundred Māori eventually signed.\n55: \n56: [Paragraph 12] One Ring\n57: The One Ring is an artefact that appears as the central plot element in J.R.R. Tolkien's The Lord of the Rings (1954 -- 55). It is described in an earlier story, The Hobbit (1937), as a magic ring of invisibility. In the sequel, The Lord of the Rings, Tolkien ascribes to the Ring a darker character, with malevolent power going far beyond conferring invisibility: it was created by Sauron the Dark Lord as part of his design to win domination over Middle - earth. The Lord of the Rings concerns the quest to destroy the Ring to keep Sauron from fulfilling his design.\n58: \n59: [Paragraph 13] Sarah McLeod\n60: Sarah McLeod (born 18 July 1971) is a New Zealand film and television actress. Her most notable role was in the Peter Jackson films The Lord of the Rings: The Fellowship of the Ring and The Lord of the Rings: The Return of the King as Rosie Cotton, a female hobbit who marries Samwise Gamgee. Her daughter Maisy played Rosie and Sam's baby. McLeod also co-starred in the New Zealand soap opera Shortland Street as Cindy Watson from 2008 to 2009.\n61: \n62: [Paragraph 14] The Lord of the G-Strings: The Femaleship of the String\n63: The Lord of the G-Strings: The Femaleship of the String is a 2003 American made for cable erotic film written and directed by Terry M. West. It is based on the novel \"The Lord of the Rings\" by J. R. R. Tolkien.\n64: \n65: [Paragraph 15] Aragorn\n66: Aragorn II, son of Arathorn is a fictional character from J. R. R. Tolkien's legendarium. He is one of the main protagonists of \"The Lord of the Rings\". Aragorn was a Ranger of the North, first introduced with the name Strider at Bree, as the Hobbits continued to call him throughout \"The Lord of the Rings\". He was eventually revealed to be the heir of Isildur and rightful claimant to the thrones of Arnor and Gondor. He was also a confidant of Gandalf and an integral part of the quest to destroy the One Ring and defeat the Dark Lord Sauron.\n67: \n68: [Paragraph 16] Elijah Wood\n\n(Showing lines 54-68 of 83. Use offset=69 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"On 6 February 1840, Hobson and about forty Māori chiefs signed the Treaty of Waitangi at Waitangi in the Bay of Islands. Copies of the Treaty were subsequently taken around the country to be signed by other chiefs. A significant number refused to sign or were not asked but, in total, more than five hundred Māori eventually signed.\n\n[Paragraph 12] One Ring\nThe One Ring is an artefact that appears as the central plot element in J.R.R. Tolkien's The Lord of the Rings (1954 -- 55). It is described in an earlier story, The Hobbit (1937), as a magic ring of invisibility. In the sequel, The Lord of the Rings, Tolkien ascribes to the Ring a darker character, with malevolent power going far beyond conferring invisibility: it was created by Sauron the Dark Lord as part of his design to win domination over Middle - earth. The Lord of the Rings concerns the quest to destroy the Ring to keep Sauron from fulfilling his design.\n\n[Paragraph 13] Sarah McLeod\nSarah McLeod (born 18 July 1971) is a New Zealand film and television actress. Her most notable role was in the Peter Jackson films The Lord of the Rings: The Fellowship of the Ring and The Lord of the Rings: The Return of the King as Rosie Cotton, a female hobbit who marries Samwise Gamgee. Her daughter Maisy played Rosie and Sam's baby. McLeod also co-starred in the New Zealand soap opera Shortland Street as Cindy Watson from 2008 to 2009.\n\n[Paragraph 14] The Lord of the G-Strings: The Femaleship of the String\nThe Lord of the G-Strings: The Femaleship of the String is a 2003 American made for cable erotic film written and directed by Terry M. West. It is based on the novel \"The Lord of the Rings\" by J. R. R. Tolkien.\n\n[Paragraph 15] Aragorn\nAragorn II, son of Arathorn is a fictional character from J. R. R. Tolkien's legendarium. He is one of the main protagonists of \"The Lord of the Rings\". Aragorn was a Ranger of the North, first introduced with the name Strider at Bree, as the Hobbits continued to call him throughout \"The Lord of the Rings\". He was eventually revealed to be the heir of Isildur and rightful claimant to the thrones of Arnor and Gondor. He was also a confidant of Gandalf and an integral part of the quest to destroy the One Ring and defeat the Dark Lord Sauron.\n\n[Paragraph 16] Elijah Wood","truncated":true,"loaded":[]},"title":"outputs/opencode_spawn_pilot/comparison_v12_single/.prompt_musique_2hop__53433_59035_1777645357.txt","time":{"start":1777645769663,"end":1777645769672}}}}
